# INT. MUNICIPAL ARCHIVE - EVENING
@ LOCATION: Municipal Archive
= Evening, fluorescent lights, almost closing time

> Rows of dented filing cabinets form narrow aisles. A wall clock reads 5:52 PM.
> MIA CHEN, 28, precise and sleep-deprived, scans a shelf label with a flashlight.

MIA
(under her breath)
Please let this be the right box.

> She pulls a heavy records box free. A corner splits and old folders spill across the floor.

OWEN
(O.S.)
Careful. Those are older than both of us.

> MIA freezes. OWEN REYES, 35, security jacket half-zipped, steps into the aisle with a ring of keys.

MIA
You lock this wing at six, right?

OWEN
That is what the giant signs suggest.

MIA
Then I have eight minutes to find permit 74-C.

OWEN
And if you do?

MIA
I prove my building did not fail inspection.

> OWEN kneels and helps gather folders.

OWEN
Seventy-four C. Waterfront district?

MIA
Block 19.

OWEN
Try overflow storage. Basement cage.

MIA
There is a basement cage?

OWEN
There is always a basement cage.

>> CUT TO:

# INT. ARCHIVE BASEMENT - MOMENTS LATER
@ LOCATION: Archive Basement
= Moments later, concrete walls, low mechanical hum

> Bare bulbs flicker above wire-mesh cages. Handwritten tags hang from rusted latches.
> OWEN unlocks one cage and swings the door open.

OWEN
You get five minutes.

MIA
I can do a lot in five minutes.

OWEN
(smiles)
That sounded threatening.

> MIA digs through stacked cartons marked with faded block numbers.

MIA
Block 17. Block 18. Holiday parade permits. Why are these with structural records?

OWEN
City planning had a "creative filing" phase.

> MIA finds a thin blue folder stamped 74-C and opens it.

MIA
No.

OWEN
No good?

MIA
No signature. The approval page is blank.

> She flips to the back and stops.

MIA
(quietly)
There is an attachment.

OWEN
What kind?

MIA
A complaint letter. Dated two days before opening.

OWEN
From who?

MIA
From my father.

> A long beat. The bulbs buzz louder in the silence.

OWEN
You did not know?

MIA
He said he signed off because the city pressured him.

OWEN
Maybe this is proof he did not.

MIA
Or proof someone made sure nobody listened.

>> SMASH CUT TO:

# EXT. ARCHIVE LOADING DOCK - NIGHT
@ LOCATION: Archive Loading Dock
= Night, light rain, sodium vapor streetlamps

> MIA and OWEN step out under a corrugated awning. Rain taps on metal.
> MIA seals the blue folder in a clear evidence bag.

OWEN
You going to the press?

MIA
First, legal. Then tenants.

OWEN
That building houses four hundred people.

MIA
I know exactly how many.

> Her phone vibrates. Caller ID: UNKNOWN.

MIA
I am not answering that.

OWEN
Probably wise.

> The call stops. A text appears: "PUT THE FILE BACK."

OWEN
You are not smiling. I am guessing that is not your mother.

MIA
Not unless she learned to threaten me in all caps.

OWEN
Then we do this properly. Chain of custody, timestamped copies, witnesses.

MIA
(nods)
I came for a permit. I found a conspiracy.

OWEN
Welcome to municipal government.

> MIA looks out at the rain, then straight ahead, decision made.

MIA
Let us make sure this one survives daylight.

>> FADE OUT.